{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And its pillars twenty.</b> Five cubits between pillar and pillar." "<b>And their sockets.</b> Of copper — the sockets rest on the ground and the pillars are set into them. He would make a kind of rod called פל\"ש בְּלַעַ\"ז, six handbreadths long and three wide, and a copper ring fixed in it at its middle, and he wraps the edge of the screen around it with cords opposite each and every pillar. And he hangs the rod by way of its ring on the hooks that are on the pillar — made like a vav: its head bent upward and its one end fixed in the pillar — like those made to set doors, which are called גונ\"ש בְּלַעַ\"ז. And the width of the screen hangs below, and it is the height of the curtains of the courtyard." "<b>And the hooks of the pillars.</b> These are the hooks." "<b>And their bands.</b> The pillars were wrapped with silver thread all around. And I do not know whether over all of them, or at their top, or in their middle — but I do know that חָשׁוּק is a term of girding, for so we find: ״וְעִמּוֹ צֶמֶד חֲמוֹרִים חֲבוּשִׁים״ (Judges 19) — its Targum: חֲשׁוּקִים."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {1 "The la'az פל\"ש is Old French pelisse or perhaps latte (slat/bar); גונ\"ש is Old French gonche/gond (door hinge/pivot)."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:54:59.901976815Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}